<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Quality Control</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To reduce individual bias, each sample is independently annotated by two annotators. Disagreements on binary veracity and fake types are resolved by a third expert who assigns the final label. We evaluate inter-annotator agreement (IoU) on grounding, achieving 0.74 for text, 0.84 for temporal spans, and 0.98 for bounding boxes. All scores exceed 70%, indicating high data quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Statistical Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GroundLie360</span> consists of 2,044 target videos under 5 minutes and 588 auxiliary videos (original, debunking, and evidence videos). Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a), (b), (f), and (g) present basic dataset statistics, including text/video length distribution and source/video type distribution.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08008v1/statistics.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/162;" width="476" height="162" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Statistic of <span id="S3.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GroundLie360</span>.<span id="S3.F3.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (d) excludes the temporal edit type from analysis, as isolated timestamps cannot be incorporated into proportion-based duration calculations.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a series of statistical analyses on dataset to provide insights into multimodal video misinformation detection. As shown in Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> <span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            Fake type counts reflect the number of videos labeled with each type; totals may exceed the number of videos due to multi-labeling. Modality counts are the sum of fake types within each modality. For instance, #V-T = #Contradictory + #Unsupported.</span></span></span>, the quantities across different modalities are relatively balanced.
Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(c), (d), (e) illustrate the distribution of grounding locations for the annotated fake segments:
In Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(c), most fake segments start near the beginning of the video. However, for the temporal edit type, fake segments are more evenly distributed across the timeline, with manipulation at the end also being common — suggesting that <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">truncated clip</span> is a frequent strategy.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(d), fake durations are generally less than half the total video length. This is especially true for false speech, where short snippets often suffice to convey a misleading narrative or claim.
Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(e) shows that most grounding bounding boxes (BBoxes) occupy a very small portion of the frame (typically &lt; 0.25). Several distinct peaks can be observed, potentially corresponding to common manipulation targets such as humans or faces.
Additionally, there is a non-negligible number of cases where the entire frame is manipulated, indicating that fully AI-generated or synthetically altered videos are also prevalent in real-world scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(h)-(k) illustrate the distribution of the number of fake types and fake segments per sample. The majority of samples (&gt;60%) contain two fake types (Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(h)), and when combined with Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(i), it suggests that cross-modality inconsistencies frequently co-occur with false textual content.
A non-negligible portion of samples (&gt;7%) contain three or more fake types, indicating the presence of more complex or multi-faceted manipulations.
The number of fake segments in text is primarily concentrated around one or two per sample (Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(j)), whereas in video (Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(k)), manipulations are limited to a single segment (&gt;80%), highlighting that single-segment tampering is the dominant pattern.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08008v1/framework.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/218;" width="476" height="218" alt="Diagram of the proposed framework for multimodal video fake news detection and grounding.">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S3.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">The workflow of <span id="S3.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FakeMark</span>.<span id="S3.F4.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (a) Overall pipeline; (b) Analysis Module, including text, video temporal/spatial, and cross-modality analyses; (c) Multimodal Classification and Grounding with a binary classifier, a multilabel classifier, and four localizers (false speech and contradictory content share designs with false title and unsupported types, respectively).</span></span><span id="acmlabel2" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">Diagram of the proposed framework for multimodal video fake news detection and grounding.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FakeMark</span>: A unified LLM-VLM framework that, given a video with its title and transcript, simultaneously predicts veracity labels, identifies misinformation types, and localizes fake content across modalities (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Problem Definition</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each instance <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,\mathbf{c},\mathbf{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,\mathbf{c},\mathbf{G})</annotation></semantics></math> contains: (1) multimodal input <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{T},\mathcal{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{T},\mathcal{R})</annotation></semantics></math> (video, title, ASR transcript); (2) veracity label <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>; (3) fake type vector <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}\in\{0,1\}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}\in\{0,1\}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> (when <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math>); (4) grounding annotations <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\{}G\}=\{G_{i}|c_{i}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\{}G\}=\{G_{i}|c_{i}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The task requires: (i) binary veracity classification; (ii) multi-label fake type prediction for fake samples; (iii) grounding localization prediction for each detected fake type.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Analysis Module</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The analysis module transforms each input sample <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> into a set of textual analyses <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are used in downstream tasks. We design four analytical prompts to address the six defined types of misinformation as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(b). <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Text analysis</span>, targeting <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">false title</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">false speech</span>, takes the title (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>) and transcript (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>) as input: <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A_{t}}=LLM(\mathcal{T},\mathcal{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>𝐭</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A_{t}}=LLM(\mathcal{T},\mathcal{R})</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video temporal analysis</span>, addressing <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">temporal edits</span>, uses the video input (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>):
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A_{vt}}=VLM(\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>𝐯𝐭</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A_{vt}}=VLM(\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video spatial analysis</span>, designed for detecting <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CGI</span>, also operates on video input: <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A_{vs}}=VLM(\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>𝐯𝐬</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A_{vs}}=VLM(\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cross-modal analysis</span>, for identifying <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contradictory content</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unsupported content</span>, takes both the title and video as input: <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A_{c}}=VLM(\mathcal{T},\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>𝐜</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A_{c}}=VLM(\mathcal{T},\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The final analysis output is represented as: <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=VLM(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{T},\mathcal{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=VLM(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{T},\mathcal{R})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Multimodal Classification and Grounding</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Binary Classifier and Multilabel Classifier.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(c1) and (c2). Given the input sample <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and its corresponding analytical results <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, the binary authenticity prediction is formulated as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=VLM(x,\mathcal{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=VLM(x,\mathcal{A})</annotation></semantics></math>, while the multilabel classification for misinformation types is expressed as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{c}}=VLM(x,\mathcal{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{c}}=VLM(x,\mathcal{A})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Textual Localizer.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is applied to four misinformation types: <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">false title</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">false speech</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unsupported content</span>, and <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contradictory content</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The grounding module takes as input the relevant textual modality (i.e., <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>) along with the corresponding analysis result (i.e., <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>𝐭</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>𝐜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>), and outputs the grounded token spans <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(c3-1) and Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(c3-2).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Temporal Edit and CGI localizer.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first perform video scene segmentation using TransNetV2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Soucek and Lokoc, 2024</a>)</cite> for two main reasons. First, for the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">temporal edit</span> type, the candidate timestamps for grounding align with scene transition points. Second, scene segmentation helps refine the grounding granularity for the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CGI</span> type.
The scene transition generation process is defined as:
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta^{t}=\text{seg}(\mathcal{V}),\quad\Theta^{t}\subseteq\{1,2,\dots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>seg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta^{t}=\text{seg}(\mathcal{V}),\quad\Theta^{t}\subseteq\{1,2,\dots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of frame indices marking scene transitions, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of video frames.
The temporal grounding process of <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">temporal edit</span> is using VLM to select misleading transitions <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(c3–3).
The CGI-type misinformation requires both temporal and spatial grounding, thus the grounding output is defined as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{G}=(\hat{\Theta},\hat{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{G}=(\hat{\Theta},\hat{B})</annotation></semantics></math>, representing the set of fake frames and bounding boxes. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.3. Statistical Analysis ‣ 3. GroundLie360 Dataset ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(c3–4), we first generate an object-level grounding query <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=\text{LLM}(\mathbf{A}_{vs})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>LLM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=\text{LLM}(\mathbf{A}_{vs})</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and scene transition frames <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the VLM selects fake scene intervals <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{s},f_{e})=\text{LLM}(\mathcal{V},q,\Theta^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>LLM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{s},f_{e})=\text{LLM}(\mathcal{V},q,\Theta^{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{s},f_{e}\in\Theta^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{s},f_{e}\in\Theta^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{s}\leq f_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{s}\leq f_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>. We uniformly sample representative frames as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta^{*}=\text{UniformSample}(f_{s},f_{e})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>UniformSample</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta^{*}=\text{UniformSample}(f_{s},f_{e})</annotation></semantics></math>. These frames are then used for initial spatial grounding: <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B^{*}=\{b_{f}\mid b_{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{4},\;f\in\Theta^{*}\}=\text{LLM}(\mathcal{V},q,\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>LLM</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{*}=\{b_{f}\mid b_{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{4},\;f\in\Theta^{*}\}=\text{LLM}(\mathcal{V},q,\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, an object tracking model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ravi et al., 2024</a>)</cite> refines both the bounding boxes and their temporal span, producing the final output: <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{G}=(\hat{\Theta},\hat{B})=\text{Track}(\Theta^{*},B^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Track</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{G}=(\hat{\Theta},\hat{B})=\text{Track}(\Theta^{*},B^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The experiments aim to answer the following research questions:
<span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q1</span>: How well do recent methods perform on classification in <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GroundLie360</span>?
<span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q2</span>: How effective is <span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FakeMark</span> for multimodal grounding?
<span id="S5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q3</span>: How challenging is the GroundMM task across different fake types?</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Experimental settings</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset Split.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate zero-shot methods on the entire Ground-Lie360 dataset. For supervised methods, the dataset is divided into 70% for training, 15% for validation, and 15% for testing.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Metrics.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use different evaluation metrics depending on the task type:
(1) <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Binary veracity classification:</span> Precision, Recall, and F1 score.
(2) <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sub-type classification:</span> Macro-averaged Precision, Recall, and F1 score.
(3) <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Grounding tasks:</span>
<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a) Textual grounding:</span> Token-level Precision, Recall, and F1 score;
<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b) Video temporal grounding:</span> Frame-level Precision, Recall, and F1 score;
<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(c) Video spatial-temporal grounding:</span> Mean temporal IoU (m_tIoU), mean visual IoU (m_vIoU), and vIoU@0.3 / vIoU@0.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation Details.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We utilize GPT-4o-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al., 2023</a>)</cite> as the LLM for text-only tasks, and InternVL-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite> as the VLM for visual and multimodal tasks. The LLM accepts up to 1024 tokens as input. For VLM video inputs, both the entire video and individual scenes obtained via scene segmentation-are uniformly sampled to 16 frames. We use TransNetV2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Soucek and Lokoc, 2024</a>)</cite> for scene segmentation and SAM2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ravi et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for object tracking.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Classification (Q1)</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Binary classification.</span> Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2. Classification (Q1) ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows binary classification performance, comparing our <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FakeMark</span> with SVFEND <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2023b</a>)</cite> and FakingRecipe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, both of which model cross-modal correlations to enhance news content representation. While <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FakeMark</span> may not outperform task-specific methods, it provides a unified, training-free framework that jointly handles multiple tasks within a single model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T3.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 3</span>. </span><span id="S5.T3.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:113%;">Binary classification results.<span id="S5.T3.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> </span>Trad.<span id="S5.T3.11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> and </span>LLM.<span id="S5.T3.11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> represent for traditional video fake detection methods and LLM-based method.</span></span></figcaption>
